The snapshot looks like late 2016 or mid-2016. Fed funds at 0.41% is consistent with 2016, before the December hike. The unemployment rate of 4.7% fits 2016, and the 10-year yield at 1.50% fits mid-2016. Gold up 9.1% in one month, long Treasuries up 6.5% in one month, and a 10-year yield down 0.28 over 3 months all point to a rally, which fits June 2016 (Brexit). Industrial production at -1.4% YoY and weak payrolls (the 3-month average of 115k reflects the May 2016 weak report) also match June 2016. Developed ex-US equity at -2.3% fits the Brexit month. The year-over-year dollar change of +3.5% also fits.

Unemployment at 4.7% in June 2016 (4.9% reported, later revised) is slightly off, but the other cues fit. Core CPI at 2.24% is consistent with mid-2016. The 10-year at 1.50% at end of June 2016 was about 1.49%, which matches.


